#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfbc2e52-a663-5cb7-98b4-0305e5f6711f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨9,1⟩+u⟨27,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387733} {:answer/id #uuid "e9630d09-3e39-5ca5-a0cd-e5ccf0c0b813" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨27,7⟩+u⟨9,7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387734} {:answer/id #uuid "026a915b-4342-55ee-ac65-fa0cfbb756c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨9,1⟩+u⟨9,7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387735} {:answer/id #uuid "3c18be1c-75ea-5a56-96c5-929ff8dd2e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨27,7⟩+u⟨14,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387736} {:answer/id #uuid "2c422e92-660c-5616-b868-8ebcf95bf214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨27,7⟩+u⟨27,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c422e92-660c-5616-b868-8ebcf95bf214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨27,7⟩+u⟨27,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387737} :answer-field/id #uuid "727d0217-ff47-55c7-97e2-76ebbeb27bcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff8b7d1f-787a-5846-b2f9-a68bcb9d4f3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-126837" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{3},{t}^{2}-2⟩$ at the point where $t=3,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=3,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(3)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(3)+u{r}^{′}(3),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =⟨{3}^{3},{3}^{2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨27,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2},2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(3) & =⟨3(3)^{2},2(3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨27,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=3$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨27,7⟩+u⟨27,6⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c0aa963-0a03-5c71-a92c-d39d53224e9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,1,-10)$ and $(-1,1,6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384831} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a862fa-4bce-59e3-8814-cae185814e4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384832} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6ef4e7-e098-539e-814c-4ab54f6240c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,4,8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384833} {:answer/id #uuid "f3017cf7-4fdd-53c0-9a64-f67616761c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384834} {:answer/id #uuid "4e647582-3df9-549a-a678-3e528cbee9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,1,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0a862fa-4bce-59e3-8814-cae185814e4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384832} :answer-field/id #uuid "75d162fc-fa25-54c2-902f-d78114c9c4d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47616907-68ba-5ff3-ae24-1153f4ea3fed" :question/math-academy-id "q-165040" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-2t\\,,\\,{t}^{2}\\,,\\,8t-2{t}^{2}⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8t-2{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩ & =c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩ & =⟨c\\,,\\,2c\\,,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t-2=c \\\\ 2t=2c \\\\ 8-4t=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we obtain that $t=2.$ Substituting to the second equation, we get $c=2.$\nThese values also satisfy the first equation. \n\nTherefore, $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨{2}^{2}-2(2)\\,,\\,{2}^{2}\\,,\\,8(2)-2(2)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,4,\\,8⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(0,4,8).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f1ed178-5cf5-5d83-a3bc-dfd7c8a21552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i-\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u(\\pi \\,i+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387740} {:answer/id #uuid "6940bd89-4ab1-5c22-ac0d-fe0022283b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u(\\pi \\,i+\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387741} {:answer/id #uuid "3e3edef2-6cc6-5021-bee2-7a90f682335e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u(\\pi \\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387742} {:answer/id #uuid "befbd5a4-c675-5da0-9912-b913adce33c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u(-\\pi \\,j-\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387743} {:answer/id #uuid "5e29cd53-1332-5338-9ca6-c391c313a8ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u(i+\\pi \\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e3edef2-6cc6-5021-bee2-7a90f682335e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u(\\pi \\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387742} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e75698a-e910-5bed-b0ad-fb527a02771e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33bfb8ae-51e5-575a-aee2-202f3434e957" :question/math-academy-id "q-32876" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j+t\\,k$ at the point $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ valid for real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2})$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r({t}_{0})+u\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =-i+0\\,j+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j+t\\,k & =-i+0\\,j+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t=-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t=0 \\\\ t=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=\\frac{3}{2}.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2})$ is at the tip of the vector $r(\\frac{3}{2}).$  \n\nWe calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{3}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i+\\pi \\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2})$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u\\,(\\pi \\,j+\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a1cf6b7-c4db-5e2a-ab3b-bdae218f32af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-7)$ and $(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384866} {:answer/id #uuid "c276f960-8bb5-5ebf-b5fe-cfaeeab8cbf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384867} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c001be-d3ba-5db9-88c3-50ac6855ef83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384868} {:answer/id #uuid "d5825a57-dd67-512c-a791-252eebf2d7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{4}{3}\\,,\\,\\frac{16}{9})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384869} {:answer/id #uuid "f451166f-d800-5ebe-be3f-3f43a56f6409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{3}\\,,\\,\\frac{16}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c276f960-8bb5-5ebf-b5fe-cfaeeab8cbf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384867} :answer-field/id #uuid "57e6bf08-2a63-5c42-a511-7482134d283f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a15bf605-19f6-54de-9516-f3cb67a15bf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-75205" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-1\\,,\\,{t}^{3}+1⟩,t\\in (-\\infty ,0)$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot 2t+1\\cdot 3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4t+3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t(4+3t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (-\\infty ,0)$, the only solution is $t=-\\frac{4}{3}$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-\\frac{4}{3}) & =⟨{(-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-1\\,,\\,{(-\\frac{4}{3})}^{3}+1⟩=⟨\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $⟨2\\,,\\,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2ee5cbf-c033-5a7a-8ae9-67d407a5777f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387747} {:answer/id #uuid "090528e6-30ba-5d9b-859c-cbc8f3855140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387748} {:answer/id #uuid "872d74c2-4fff-5731-82a9-452b9fc6e3ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387749} {:answer/id #uuid "8f2afec9-7f6c-5645-af5d-df036d1b6c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3)$ and $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387750} {:answer/id #uuid "4447b138-bebf-5afe-b78d-5672fa327263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2ee5cbf-c033-5a7a-8ae9-67d407a5777f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387747} :answer-field/id #uuid "a25f1b88-efd6-5bc5-adff-d60834a59ef9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f1e2ae0c-272c-5ed9-a620-4462d5cc1c06" :question/math-academy-id "q-75524" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,t\\in [0,\\pi )$ find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =0 \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in [0,\\pi )$, the only solutions are $t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=⟨0\\,,\\,3⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩=⟨1\\,,\\,0⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the points $(0,3)$ and $(1,0).$"}]}
